 Top of the Morning 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Photo: Ben Hider/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Wall Street isn't sweating existential threats to humanity. That's a tomorrow problem. Today there's money to make.

 Driving the news: Anthropic last night raised $81.35 billion in its IPO, after pricing 980 million shares at $83 per share. It had planned to sell 825 million shares at $70-$78 per share.

 This is expected to be the largest IPO ever, topping SpaceX, assuming that underwriters exercise their greenshoes. And that's not even including a concurrent $26 billion private placement. Anthropic's initial market value would be nearly $1.1 trillion, with a fully diluted value of around $1.5 trillion. But all of that will be moot once shares begin trading, with a pop all but guaranteed. ROI: Anthropic was valued at $965 billion after its most recent private round, led by Dragoneer, Coatue, Sequoia Capital, Greenoaks, and Altimeter.

 It first raised venture money in early 2021 at a $461 million post-money valuation led by Skype co-founder Jaan Tallinn. Sam Bankman-Fried (yes, that one) led the Series B round one year later at a $3 billion post-money valuation. In 2023, Anthropic was valued at $4.6 billion after a Series C round led by Spark Capital, Salesforce Ventures, Zoom Ventures, and Alphabet. The big picture: It's too early to know if this is just FOMO or a sincere belief that AI trade tumult is really in the rearview.

 But it's nonetheless a remarkable achievement. The Trump administration in March tried to kneecap Anthropic by broadly designating it a "supply chain risk," but courts later limited the impact. Two months ago, Anthropic's own CEO wrote about "pacing the frontier." OpenAI pushed its own IPO into 2027 and lots of folks fretted that the Claude maker would follow suit. Then came the management mess at Nvidia, one of Anthropic's major investors and strategic partners. Brief bull case: Demand is to infinity and beyond. Anthropic beat its top rival to the public markets and now has tons of cash to pay for compute and build up its application layer via acquisition. It also has a glide path to the indices, and had a roadshow chart claiming that the company is learning to produce intelligence with less compute.

 Brief bear case: Enterprise's eyes might have been bigger than their stomachs for AI spend, particularly with open-source alternatives, and the nearly $900 billion in compute obligations remain onerous. Plus, its top rival gets to continue operating as a private company — without the public market's glare or reporting obligations.

 The bottom line: This is one IPO for one company, but the tech ecosystem ripples are sure to be wide.

 It's sure to mint thousands of new millionaires, many of whom will go on to become angel investors. Eligible employees can sell up to 10% of vested shares on Day 1. It also should lead to record distributions for a huge swath of VC funds — many of which also were in SpaceX — thus keeping institutional investors in the LP game. In short, the startup flywheel keeps spinning. 
 
